<Base title="About — Tomás Campoy Rojo" description="About Tomás Campoy Rojo, a Data Engineer focused on data platforms, cloud architecture, AI systems, automation and production reliability.">
  <section class="page-intro"><div class="eyebrow">About</div><h1>I like building systems that make the next decision easier.</h1><p>I’m Tomás Campoy Rojo, a Data Engineer working across data platforms, cloud architecture, AI systems and automation.</p></section>
  <article class="article">
    <p class="kicker">My default mode is to reduce ambiguity: define the source of truth, make transformations reproducible, expose quality and failure modes, and give downstream users or agents a stable contract.</p>
    <h2>What I do</h2>
    <p>I work on engineering problems that sit between raw operational systems and the people, products or models that need reliable information: ingestion, transformation, orchestration, analytical datasets, automation, performance and platform design.</p>
    <p>My current professional work is at Universidad Católica San Antonio de Murcia (UCAM), where I work on institutional data and analytics systems. I also build public data-engineering projects, experiment with AI-agent infrastructure and take on selected technical collaborations outside that context.</p>
    <h2>Technical focus</h2>
    <p>My core data stack is Python, Polars, SQL, Airflow, dbt, Spark and Databricks, with cloud work across AWS, Azure and GCP. I’m especially interested in lakehouse architecture, CDC, streaming, orchestration, data quality and observability.</p>
    <p>On the AI-systems side, I’m interested in the engineering around the model: context assembly, retrieval, model routing, tool execution, MCP, durable workflows, sandboxes, verification, tracing and evals. The model matters; the harness determines whether the system is actually dependable.</p>
    <h2>How I work</h2>
    <p>I prefer measurable outcomes to technology theatre: correctness before cleverness, explicit trade-offs, performance that can be benchmarked, systems that are easy to inspect when they fail, and documentation that explains why the architecture exists.</p>
    <h2>Certifications</h2>
    <ul><li>AWS Certified Data Engineer – Associate</li><li>Azure Databricks Platform Architect</li></ul>
    <h2>Open to</h2>
    <p>Interesting data-engineering roles, AI-infrastructure work, technical collaborations, consulting projects and conversations with teams building serious data or agent systems. The best fit is usually a problem with real constraints, measurable impact and room to own the engineering decisions.</p>
  </article>
